# Classic static include of the theme, for installations that do not use the
# site set. It imports the very same file the set reads.
#
# The condition is what makes the two mechanisms safe to have side by side.
# Site sets and sys_template records are processed by separate code paths with
# no cross check, and the static include is appended AFTER the set. Without the
# condition, a site that uses both would parse the theme twice, and the second
# pass would silently overwrite every customisation made on top of the shipped
# defaults - which presents as "my site setting does nothing", not as visible
# duplication.
#
# "site('sets')" lists the sets a site declares itself, so a theme pulled in as
# a transitive dependency of another set is not covered here. Enabling the theme
# through its own set, which is the documented way, is.
#
# The "?: []" is not defensive noise. Site sets do not exist before TYPO3 v13.1
# (#103437), so on v12 "site('sets')" resolves a method the Site entity does not
# have and returns NULL. Symfony compiles "in" to "in_array($left, $right,
# true)", which raises a TypeError for NULL - and
# "IncludeTreeConditionMatcherVisitor" catches only SyntaxError and
# RuntimeException, so the error is not contained: every frontend request that
# loads this file dies. Falling back to an empty array makes the condition
# answer "no set is active" on v12, which is the only truthful answer there.
# @todo Drop the fallback when TYPO3 v12 support is dropped.
[not ('sbuerk/theme-extension-development' in (site('sets') ?: []))]
    @import 'EXT:theme_extension_development/Configuration/TypoScript/setup.typoscript'
[END]
